WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.880
Many sci-fi fans have long hoped for a version of&nbsp;
"Dune" that would do greater visual and narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.080
justice to Frank Herbert's work. Now, director&nbsp;
Denis Villeneuve has been drafted to bring it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.760
to the big-screen. Here's everything we know&nbsp;
about the 2021 adaptation of “Dune” so far.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.240
The road to making a new cinematic&nbsp;
version of "Dune" was lengthy even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.640
before the current roster of talent signed&nbsp;
on for the film. Director Denis Villeneuve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.160
who'd long hoped to helm a new adaptation of&nbsp;
the novel, signed on to make the film in 2017.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.760
However, it wasn't until two years later that&nbsp;
the project actually landed a 2020 release date.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.520
Then the COVID-19 pandemic hit, making a 2020&nbsp;
release for "Dune" very unlikely and leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.800
Warner Bros. to make some new moves. In October of&nbsp;
2020, Warner Bros. announced that the film would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.880
shift to October 1, 2021. Then, in December,&nbsp;
the studio dropped some even bigger news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.280
Its entire 2021 film slate, including "Dune,"&nbsp;
would also simultaneously premiere on the HBO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.240
Max streaming service, giving viewers a chance&nbsp;
to watch the film at home without waiting.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:07.440
“It’s time.”

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.800
Any "Dune" adaptation faces a monumental challenge&nbsp;
in assembling a cast to fill Herbert's universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.680
It starts with finding the perfect actors to&nbsp;
stand in the center of it all as the Atreides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.640
family. In 2018, Oscar nominee Timothée&nbsp;
Chalamet joined the film as Paul Atreides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.760
making Dune his first blockbuster role. Shortly&nbsp;
after reports of Chalamet's involvement started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.560
circulating, Rebecca Ferguson joined the film&nbsp;
as Paul's mother, the Bene Gesserit woman Lady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.080
Jessica. She first begins teaching him the gifts&nbsp;
that will later manifest as messianic abilities.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.480
With the two most prominent members of House&nbsp;
Atreides in place, it was time for the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.360
to cast Duke Leto Atreides. This is Paul's&nbsp;
father who brings his family to Arrakis to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.160
take over leadership of the planet, and finds&nbsp;
only trouble. In early 2019, it was revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.160
that Duke Leto would be played by Oscar Isaac, who&nbsp;
adds another major sci-fi franchise to his resume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.600
just a few years after joining the "Star Wars"&nbsp;
galaxy. These three actors form the core family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.720
unit that is House Atreides, but they're&nbsp;
far from the only major stars in the film.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:18.880
"Dune" is an epic novel spanning several years&nbsp;
and featuring two major royal houses, Atreides and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.760
Harkonnen, who battle each other for supremacy on&nbsp;
the planet. One that also includes its own native&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.440
race, the Fremen. That means a massive cast&nbsp;
needed to be assembled to make the film work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.040
Because this is a Denis Villeneuve film adaptation&nbsp;
of one of the most beloved genre novels ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.400
A-list actors have lined up to make the supporting&nbsp;
cast for "Dune" a truly all-star ensemble.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.720
Among the stars assembled to round out&nbsp;
the film's cast are Stellan Skarsgard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.600
Jason Momoa, Javier Bardem, Zendaya, Josh&nbsp;
Brolin, David Dastmalchian, Dave Bautista,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.720
and Charlotte Rampling. It's an exciting,&nbsp;
diverse roundup of some of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.400
acclaimed actors working right now in both&nbsp;
action blockbusters and prestige dramas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.600
It’s exactly the right blend for Herbert's&nbsp;
sprawling space opera of warring dynasties.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.040
"Dune" is one of the most celebrated&nbsp;
and influential science fiction novels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.880
ever written. First published in 1965, Frank&nbsp;
Herbert's novel is set amid a future civilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.920
built on a kind of interplanetary feudal system.&nbsp;
There's a central government led by an Emperor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.360
and various planets are controlled by his&nbsp;
vassals, which take the title of Baron or Duke.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.840
The main action of "Dune" takes place on&nbsp;
the planet Arrakis. It’s one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:40.560
important fiefdoms in the Empire because its vast&nbsp;
deserts contain "the spice," an addictive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.800
valuable substance. The novel follows the&nbsp;
Atreides family as they are given control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.720
of Arrakis by the Emperor, only to find that a&nbsp;
vast plot to overthrow them is already underway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.840
Amid these political struggles, Duke Leto&nbsp;
Atreides' young son, Paul, begins to manifest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.720
superhuman abilities that make him a messiah&nbsp;
figure among the local Fremen. A rebellion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.240
begins, and Paul's abilities make him into a&nbsp;
leader fit to challenge the Emperor himself.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.760
“My lord, Duke.”

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.440
Of course, Herbert's novel is&nbsp;
much more complicated than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:17.440
and after "Dune" he just kept going. Herbert&nbsp;
himself wrote five sequels to the novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.360
and concluded his series with "Chapterhouse:&nbsp;
Dune" in 1985, but the story didn't end there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.320
Herbert's son Brian picked up the torch from&nbsp;
his father and, with novelist Kevin J. Anderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.360
has published more than a dozen more "Dune" books,&nbsp;
many of them prequels to the original novel.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.120
The earliest attempts to adapt&nbsp;
Dune for the screen kicked off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:43.360
just a few years after the novel was published.&nbsp;
In the mid-1970s, director Alejandro Jodorowsky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.440
launched a tremendously ambitious&nbsp;
effort to bring the novel to the screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.600
The project involved everyone from Orson Welles to&nbsp;
Salvador Dalí to Pink Floyd. Jodorowsky eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.840
abandoned the project when the financing&nbsp;
for the film could not be fully realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.120
but his efforts were later chronicled in&nbsp;
the 2013 documentary "Jodorowsky's Dune."

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.120
The post-"Star Wars" era improved the&nbsp;
fortunes of "Dune" as a movie. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.320
in 1984 a big-screen version helmed by director&nbsp;
David Lynch was finally released, starring Kyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.920
MacLachlan as Paul Atreides. Although it has since&nbsp;
become a cult film, "Dune" was a box office flop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:23.120
and earned negative reviews. Lynch, not yet the&nbsp;
household name he would be after "Twin Peaks,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:28.960
was frustrated by a lack of creative control&nbsp;
and eventually disowned his "Dune" altogether.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.760
A longer and more faithful adaptation of Herbert's&nbsp;
novel arrived on the small screen in 2000 as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:38.080
"Frank Herbert's Dune." It’s a TV miniseries&nbsp;
written by John Harrison that aired in three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.320
two-hour installments on the Sci-Fi Channel.&nbsp;
It was successful enough that a sequel, "Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.760
Herbert's Children of Dune," followed in 2003 and&nbsp;
adapted the next two novels in Herbert's series.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.360
Attempts to bring "Dune" back to the big screen&nbsp;
kicked into gear in 2008, when Paramount Pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.920
announced Peter Berg would direct a new&nbsp;
adaptation. That effort eventually stalled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.600
and in 2016 Legendary Pictures announced that&nbsp;
they had landed the film and TV rights for "Dune."

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.400
Soon after Legendary's announcement that they&nbsp;
would be adapting "Dune" for the screen again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.880
reports began to circulate that director Denis&nbsp;
Villeneuve was the studio's choice to direct the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.680
new film version. Villeneuve's involvement&nbsp;
was confirmed by Brian Herbert in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.880
after he dazzled audiences with "Arrival"&nbsp;
but before "Blade Runner 2049" arrived in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.480
theaters to critical acclaim. Thanks&nbsp;
to those two films in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.960
Villeneuve is one of the most important&nbsp;
voices in science fiction cinema right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.640
He's a lifelong "Dune" fan who called the chance&nbsp;
to adapt the book the project of his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:42.000
But he made it clear that he wouldn't be relying&nbsp;
on the Lynch film version at all. Villeneuve said,

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.720
"I mean, David Lynch is one of the best&nbsp;
filmmakers alive. I have massive respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.280
for him, but when I saw his adaptation, I was&nbsp;
impressed, but it was not what I had dreamed of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.000
so I'm trying to make the adaptation of my&nbsp;
dreams. It will not have any link with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:01.840
David Lynch movie. I'm going back to the book, and&nbsp;
going to the images that came out when I read it."

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.280
Frank Herbert's original novel is not the longest&nbsp;
genre epic you're likely to find on the shelves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:13.040
at your local bookstores, but it is among the&nbsp;
most dense. "Dune" is a book absolutely packed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:20.240
with plot, covering dozens of characters, multiple&nbsp;
groups and cultures, and a vast mythology — some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:25.600
of which is only hinted at in the first novel.&nbsp;
It's the kind of book so full of detail that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:30.320
needs an appendix at the back so you can look&nbsp;
up various invented terms for more clarity.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:34.720
That means adapting the book to the screen is&nbsp;
hard, particularly when you're working within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:39.520
the constraints of a big-budget film. These&nbsp;
days that pretty much demands you weigh in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:45.040
with a runtime of three hours or less. Even&nbsp;
with that in mind, though, adapting "Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:50.400
as one three-hour story is a daunting prospect&nbsp;
that's defeated filmmakers in the past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:56.640
So, for Villeneuve's film version, the solution&nbsp;
was obvious: don't try to make "Dune" one film.

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.440
Villeneuve made it clear that he was setting a&nbsp;
goal of two films for "Dune." In the summer of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.720
2018, Brian Herbert confirmed that the screenplay&nbsp;
for the new film would adapt approximately half of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.000
the first novel in the series. It's not clear&nbsp;
how far "approximately half" will take us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.960
but the novel does include a time jump to allow&nbsp;
Paul Atreides to age a bit at one point, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.680
it's possible the first film will leave off there.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.320
Brian Herbert confirmed in 2018 that&nbsp;
Villeneuve's desire to split "Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:28.720
into two films had come to fruition, and that&nbsp;
the film we'd see first would only adapt half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.920
the novel. This was reaffirmed in the spring&nbsp;
of 2019 by Legendary Pictures CEO Joshua Grode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:37.520
who added a little context as to&nbsp;
why the split happened. He shared,

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.080
"There's a backstory that was hinted at in&nbsp;
some of the books [that we expanded]. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.040
when you read the book there's a logical place&nbsp;
to stop the movie before the book is over."

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.080
There's one really interesting&nbsp;
wrinkle in all of this, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.200
Yes, "Dune" will be split into two films,&nbsp;
but it's not like "The Lord of the Rings."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.480
Peter Jackson shot all of&nbsp;
those movies back-to-back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.920
but Villeneuve is not making one "Dune" film&nbsp;
right after the other. Instead, Legendary seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.160
to be waiting to see how the first film fares&nbsp;
before launching into production on a sequel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.120
This adds intrigue to what Villeneuve said when&nbsp;
he first discussed his two-movie plan. He said,

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:19.200
"'Dune will probably take two years to make.&nbsp;
The goal is to make two films, maybe more."

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.360
So far, the only adaptation we've gotten beyond&nbsp;
Herbert's first novel is the "Children of Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:29.120
miniseries. However, if Villeneuve has his way we&nbsp;
could one day see a film version of other books.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:34.000
After years of anticipation, the&nbsp;
first official trailer for "Dune"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.080
arrived in September of 2020. This was&nbsp;
back when Warner Bros. was still holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.160
out hope that the film might make it into&nbsp;
theaters that December. That, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:47.680
didn't happen. But the trailer still managed&nbsp;
to capture the hearts and imaginations of fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.640
raising the anticipation level for the film&nbsp;
more than a year before its eventual release.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:58.400
The first trailer is much more of a teaser than&nbsp;
a full plot breakdown. It spends much of its time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.800
with Paul Atreides as he grapples with visions&nbsp;
of what might happen to him and his family as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:08.480
they head for the desert planet of Arrakis. We&nbsp;
see him tested, tormented, and doing his best to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:13.600
be strong even as the dangers of the planet&nbsp;
become apparent. The trailer is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.720
packed with dazzling visuals, leading up&nbsp;
to the reveal of a sandworm at the end.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:23.840
The second official trailer, released in the&nbsp;
summer of 2021, digs more heavily into the plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:29.680
and the supporting cast of the film, and pays&nbsp;
particular attention to Chani, the Fremen girl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:34.640
Paul eventually falls in love with. In this&nbsp;
trailer we get a much better sense of the plot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.600
Paul begins to realize not only that his family&nbsp;
is in grave danger from the evil Harkonnens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.120
but that he himself might not actually be the&nbsp;
person to lead House Atreides into the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.880
It's a much fuller view of the film,&nbsp;
one that allows us to see more of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:53.840
internal dynamics of the ensemble, as&nbsp;
well as some great action sequences.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:57.680
“Get everything with guns off the ground. Go”

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:03.440
Not all were happy when WarnerMedia announced&nbsp;
“Dune” would simultaneously premiere in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.880
theaters and on the HBO Max streaming service,&nbsp;
including "Dune" director Denis Villeneuve.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.680
In a statement released to Variety at the time,&nbsp;
Villeneuve blasted Warners' parent company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.120
AT&amp;T. He explained that he learned&nbsp;
about the release plans, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:20.000
"in the news" like everyone else. He wrote,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:26.240
"With this decision AT&amp;T has hijacked one of&nbsp;
the most respectable and important studios in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:32.080
film history. There is absolutely no love&nbsp;
for cinema, nor for the audience here."

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.520
Though Villeneuve was not the only&nbsp;
critic of the release strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:40.400
he has remained among the loudest. In an&nbsp;
August 2021 interview with “Total Film,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.960
he called the move ridiculous and regarding&nbsp;
watching "Dune" on a home TV, said,

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.360
"[It’s like] driv[ing] a&nbsp;
speedboat in your bathtub."

